body:
While browsers increasing support the `loading` attribute for lazy-loading images, this does not apply to CSS background-images. As a result, developers often need to build a [custom solution](https://web.dev/lazy-loading-images/#:~:text=From%20here%2C%20use%20JavaScript%20to%20check%20if%20the%20element%20is%20in%20the%20viewport%20(with%20Intersection%20Observer!)%2C%20and%20add%20the%20visible%20class%20to%20the%20div.lazy%2Dbackground%20element%20at%20that%20time%2C%20which%20loads%20the%20image%3A) to address this problem that relies on Intersection Observer and JS. We also need a solution to this problem for content management platforms (e.g. WordPress) where lazy-loading is now baked in by default.

I ran into this problem again today and wanted to restart discussions on whether this is a worthwhile problem to solve. I don't have strong opinions on the exact shape of how we may wish to enable loading configuration for background-image URLs, but can throw out a few suggestions. I'm sure others will have wiser ideas :)

```css
.box-1 {
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255,255,0,0.5), rgba(0,0,255,0.5)),
    url('catfront.png');
  /* Setting loading behavior via background-image-loading */
  background-image-loading: "lazy";
}

.box-2 {
  /* Setting loading behavior via loading() */
  background-image: url("large-balloons.jpg") loading("lazy");
  background-image: image-set(
    url("large-balloons.avif") type("image/avif"),
    url("large-balloons.jpg") type("image/jpeg"));
}
```

Please feel free to close if we already have an issue for discussing lazy-loading background  images elsewhere. I was unable to locate one.

cc @zcorpan @yoavweiss @chrishtr 

References:

- https://web.dev/browser-level-image-lazy-loading/#can-css-background-images-take-advantage-of-the-loading-attribute
- https://web.dev/lazy-loading-images/#images-css
- https://imagekit.io/blog/lazy-loading-images-complete-guide/#lazy-loading-css-background-images
